# The user runs all commands, not the agent

This machine's sandbox is unreliable for this project (Docker Desktop/Windows
socket access, network flakiness, corrupted caches - see the yarn/Traefik
incidents in chat history). Do not run shell commands yourself, even with
elevated permissions. Instead:

1. Give the user the **exact, copy-pasteable command(s)** in a fenced code
   block (`powershell` or `bash` as appropriate for their shell).
2. Explain briefly what each command does and what output/result to expect.
3. Ask the user to run it and paste back the output (or describe what
   happened) before you continue.
4. Do not call the `Shell` tool to execute app/infra commands (`yarn`,
   `docker`, `git` push/commit-affecting commands, service start/stop, etc.)
   on the user's behalf.

## Exceptions (still fine to run yourself)

- Read-only inspection that doesn't depend on a working sandbox and isn't the
  thing being debugged: `Read`, `Grep`, `Glob`, and read-only `git status` /
  `git diff` / `git log` (no `-i`, no mutating flags) when you need repo state
  to write a plan or docs - not when the user is actively trying to reproduce
  or fix a runtime/build issue themselves.
- If the user explicitly asks you to run something yourself in a given
  message, you may do so for that request only; this rule resumes
  immediately after.

## Why

The user wants to be the one executing `docker compose`, `yarn`, and similar
commands on their own machine so they can see real output directly, control
timing, and avoid this sandbox's Docker/network limitations producing
misleading results.
